()):\n                    can_add = False\n                    break\n            \n            if can_add:\n                # Recursively explore by adding the candidate\n                new_clique = current_clique + [candidate]\n                new_remaining = [\n                    r for r in remaining_candidates[i+1:]\n                    if r in graph.edges.get(candidate, set()) or len(new_clique) == 0 # Optimize remaining candidates\n                ]\n                # Optimization: Only consider candidates that are connected to ALL current_clique members\n                # The `new_remaining` should contain nodes that can extend `new_clique`.\n                # For a node `r` to be a valid `new_remaining` candidate for `new_clique`,\n                # it must be connected to `candidate` and all existing `current_clique` members.\n                # However, the previous `can_add` ensures `candidate` connects to `current_clique`.\n                # So `r` just needs to connect to `candidate`.\n                \n                # A more precise optimization for the Bron-Kerbosch-like algorithm:\n                # `new_remaining` are nodes that are connected to `candidate`\n                # AND all nodes already in `new_clique`.\n                # Simpler: `new_remaining` are neighbors of `candidate` in `remaining_candidates`\n                # (and implicitly already connected to current_clique if they were in original remaining_candidates)\n\n                # A basic backtracking that just checks validity with the Subset class:\n                # `new_remaining = remaining_candidates[i+1:]`\n                # This is simpler and directly uses the graph.edges and isValid for verification.\n\n                find_cliques(new_clique, remaining_candidates[i+1:]) # Explore with candidate and further elements\n\n    # Initial call to the backtracking function\n    # Sort nodes to potentially prune faster or explore in a consistent order.\n    # The actual values o